Methodology
The project explores the role of market sentiment in driving the behavior and performance of the cryptocurrency traders using the Fear-Greed Index and historical trading data. Sentiment labels were initially matched with individual trade activity and the datasets were initially cleaned and sorted by date. The behavioral patterns were then computed by calculating the daily trader-level statistics, that is, profit/loss (PnL), trade frequency, and average trade size.

To determine the performance of traders in different sentiment conditions and to analyze the variation in the trading activity in different market emotion, exploratory data analysis was conducted. A random forest model was trained to classify the categories of profitability of traders daily based on sentiment and behavioral characteristics. Also, KMeans clustering was used to divide traders into behavioral archetypes in terms of activity, trade size, and profitability.

Key Insights
The trading performance and behaviour were shown in the analysis to depend heavily on the market sentiment. During fear-based market situations when it is in an extreme fear situation, the level of trading activity is higher and this shows reactive and possibly impulsive trading activity. The highest performance variation is recorded in extreme fear and extreme greed state, so it is proposed that markets that are emotionally charged result in increased risk taking and unpredictable performance.

There was segmentation of the trader in terms of their high activity and larger sizes of the trade where the traders who are active and have larger trade are more likely to incur higher profitability because they are able to have larger exposure to the market. On the other hand, low-activity traders are lower with smaller position that exhibit low and consistent returns. It was one of the large factors that affect the profitability outcomes, as both the frequency of the trade and the size of positions were identified.

Strategy Recommendations
Through the findings, a number of actionable insights may be drawn. Extreme fear conditions do not permit traders to engage excessively in trading because emotional markets tend to become more risky and the decision will be impulsive. It seems more effective to adopt systematic high-frequency trading and heavy risk management practice than periodic trading behavior. There should also be a careful use of aggressive leverage in times of high volatility and market sentiment due to the high uncertainty of its performance.
